ng into recession shouldn't  some of those issues be taken into  consideration and climate perhaps  sometimes has to take a backseat  we can absolutely have climate as a  priority alongside uh labor and  environmental protections and we must do  that um if we don't bake in these  protections then uh what's um what we're  putting ourselves at risk of happening  is that um uh all these industries that  we're that we're trying to develop will  offshore their production offshore their  jobs to places where it's um easier and  to pay people less to places where it's  easier to pollute more  and then as a result you have green  trade on paper and  extremely dirty uh trade and reality so  we absolutely must bake those  protections in to protect our jobs as  well as to protect the climate  and tom do you welcome what the  trade international trade secretary is  saying today talking about wanting to  lead the way on green trade there's talk  of nearly 190 million pounds of new loan  guarantees of new jobs being created  helping heavily polluting countries like  india for example sharing expertise on  wind power i mean it sounds like heading  in the right direction does it to you  it is the right direction there's no  doubt about that but but we always  find at the moment from the government  that it talks a bigger talk than it acts  and its actions are always rather less  uh dramatic than its headlines  and and it's fine to be pointing in the  right direction but the really important  thing is the report that came out today  from the world uh metrological  organization made clear is we really are  running out of time so what we'd like  i'd like to see is is not only getting  in the right direction but moving faster  in that direction  and anna i know at the tc obviously  you're going to be very concerned about  jobs and  they're making it clear today that green  trade spells green jobs but some people  working in fossil fuel industries might  say but i'm really worried about losing  my job i mean is there a balance that we  need to strike there  what we need to do is we need to future  proof the high carbon industries that we  already have so we've calculated that  there's about um six hundred thousand um  660 000 uh manufacturing and supply  chain jobs in the uk that depend on  um on on action to future proof these  industries on public and private  investment  and on trade deals that protect these  jobs from being offshored  so there's absolutely no either or we  just have to make a plan for how these  industries transition  to the net zero aligned business model  and we'd have to do it on time and we  have to have trade deals that protect  those jobs in the meantime  and tommy are you confident that kind of  transition is in place that we're on  track  no i'm not confident we're on track but  i agree very much with what anna was  saying if you look at the best possible  thing you could do for the climate in  this country right now is invest in  improving energy efficiency uh which  would cut bills create lots of jobs uh  and really help and that opportunity was  completely missed in the spring budget  for instance and in the energy security  strategy if you want to get off using  russian gas the quickest way you can do  it is to reduce the demand for it by  keeping build people's bills down your  energy efficiency and i don't need to  ask you what you think about that cause  i can see you nodding away there so we  will move on to the next topic if that's  all right and uh the numbers are pretty  astonishing one in six deaths in the  world are caused in part at least by  pollution and of those air pollution is  the biggest killer and tom these figures  really are extraordinary and perhaps  what's even more shocking is the fact  that they haven't really changed uh  since 2015. so why do you think that  such a big killer is not being tackled  more effectively  uh i wish i knew why because not only is  it awfully  bad for people and people's health  especially children especially anybody  who's got a problem like asthma and so  on but but much more importantly it  praises an enormous burden on an already  overstretched health service that we  don't deal with this problem in  particular the bit that we really need  to do uh in our cities especially as we  know the summer coming on we really do  need to speed up the  pace at which we take out the internal  combustion engine and replace it with  electric vehicles which don't create  that local urban pollution which is a  big source of the problem for people  and anna the report said that 90 of  pollution deaths occur in low and middle  income countries i mean more generally  speaking is it poor people who are  suffering the most  yes absolutely and uh we know that um  bad air quality disproportionately  affects uh working-class communities and  it also disproportionately affects even  in the uk also disproportionately um  affects um black and i think minority  communities as well  um but the  the the good thing that i would like to  say is that we can all do quite a lot on  this and it's not just political action  it's not just demanding action of our  political representatives it's not just  individual action but it's also action  in our workplaces where air pollution  affects everybody at work um on some  level and uh we should all be thinking  of ways that we can tackle that problem  for example by switching our workplaces  fleets to electric um cars where that's  possible  so so basically what you're saying anna  is that employers can make some really  sharp decisions that will protect their  employees  absolutely yes we've seen that happen at  royal mail for example they've committed  to a fully electric fleet by 2030  and they've got a really good process as  well with the postal workers union the  cwu where they're working out on a  localized level how to best arrange this  so that people are protected from the  risks associated with air pollution and  so that it works for the post users as  well  and tom interesting there that anna's  talking about you know companies  switching to electric fleets for example  and does that make you think this  problem could sort itself out in the  drive towards net zero as we move off  polluting industries as we move away  from polluting vehicles for example  that will  reduce pollution  well it will certainly reduce pollution  but i don't know  from the climate let alone for people's  health that we're going fast enough but  the thing i think we miss and anna was  right to point out what companies can do  and what people can do in their own  lives but we should be making much more  use of local government as a delivery  mechanism for improving air quality as  we found out during the pandemic local  government turned out to be a really  important part of being able to deal  with a sort of  crisis like this that's very dispersed  and different slightly  from area to area and i think we've got  a whole  sort of approach to this i think you  must do things if government's got to do  them it's always got to be national  government and i think we underestimate  the importance of local government  particularly when you've got to try to  get people to join in and  do bits themselves local government is  much better at getting people to do  things the national government is  interesting okay tom and anna we are out  of time but uh very interesting to get  your thoughts today thanks very much  indeed  so do stay with us coming up next we've  got the take with sophie ridge and  attorney general suella braverman  discusses the government's response to  the cost of living crisis that's coming  up next but from me that's it thanks for  watching and i'll see you next time  [Music]
 

